---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FindAndReplace from '@/components/tools/text/FindAndReplace.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="find-and-replace">
  <FindAndReplace client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to find and replace text</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste your text, or drop a file onto the box.</li>
      <li>Type the search text. The match count and the highlighted preview update as you type, before anything changes.</li>
      <li>Type the replacement. Leave it empty to delete all matches.</li>
      <li>Switch to regular-expression mode when you need patterns, not literal text.</li>
      <li>Check the before/after preview and the match table, then copy, download, or press <strong>Apply to input</strong> to continue editing.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Plain text mode</h2>
    <p>
      Plain mode matches exactly what you type, with no special characters to escape. Two features are still
      available in both fields: escape sequences (<code>\n</code> for a line break, <code>\t</code> for a
      tab, <code>\uXXXX</code> for any Unicode character) and the whole-word option, which stops
      <code>cat</code> from matching inside <code>category</code>. A literal <code>$1</code> in the
      replacement stays a literal <code>$1</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Regular expressions and capture groups</h2>
    <p>
      Regex mode uses the JavaScript flavour, including lookahead, lookbehind, named groups and Unicode
      property escapes. Text in parentheses is captured, and you can reference it in the replacement.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Goal</th>
            <th>Find</th>
            <th>Replace with</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Renumber invoices</td><td><code>Invoice (\d+)</code></td><td><code>INV-$1</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Swap “Last, First” to “First Last”</td><td><code>(\w+), (\w+)</code></td><td><code>$2 $1</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>US date to ISO</td><td><code>{'(\\d{2})/(\\d{2})/(\\d{4})'}</code></td><td><code>$3-$1-$2</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Delete trailing spaces</td><td><code>[ \t]+$</code></td><td>(empty)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Collapse repeated blank lines</td><td><code>{'\\n{3,}'}</code></td><td><code>\n\n</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Strip HTML tags</td><td><code>&lt;[^&gt;]+&gt;</code></td><td>(empty)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wrap every line in quotes</td><td><code>^(.+)$</code></td><td><code>"$1",</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Keep only the domain</td><td><code>.*@(.+)</code></td><td><code>$1</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Special replacement tokens follow the JavaScript standard: <code>$&amp;</code> is the whole match,
      <code>$1</code>–<code>$99</code> are numbered groups, <code>$&lt;name&gt;</code> is a named group, and
      <code>$$</code> inserts a literal dollar sign.
    </p>

    <h2>The option switches</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Option</th>
            <th>Effect</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Case sensitive</td><td>When off, adds the <code>i</code> flag, so ACME, Acme and acme all match.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Whole words only</td><td>Requires a non-word character (or the start/end of the text) on both sides of the match.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multiline</td><td>Adds the <code>m</code> flag, so <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> anchor to each line, not the whole text.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dot matches newline</td><td>Adds the <code>s</code> flag, so <code>.</code> also matches line breaks. Needed for patterns that span lines.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Why the preview matters</h2>
    <p>
      A bad regex can destroy a document without warning. The before/after panels highlight your matches in
      place, and the match table lists the first twenty matches with the exact replacement and the line of
      each. Nothing changes in the input box until you press <strong>Apply to input</strong>, so you can try
      patterns safely. If the pattern is invalid, the tool shows the error from the engine.
    </p>

    <h2>Handling large files</h2>
    <p>
      Matching runs in chunks, and the interface repaints between them, so a file of several megabytes stays
      responsive. The highlighted preview shows only the first few thousand characters, for speed; the result
      box and the download always contain the complete text. All processing happens in your browser, so
      confidential documents stay on your machine.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
